CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Act is to protect the rights and interests of livestock farmers and consumers and to promote the stable development of the livestock farming industry by prescribing matters necessary for the creation and operation of self-help funds for livestock farming.

Article 2 (Definitions) The definitions of the terms used in this Act are as follows: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 31, 2014>
  1. The term "livestock product" means meat, milk, eggs, and other products of livestock prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs produced from cattle, pigs, chickens, etc.;
  2. The term "livestock farmer" means a person who produces livestock products in the Republic of Korea or raises livestock for the production of such livestock products;
  3. The term "livestock organization" means a non-profit-making juristic person established pursuant to Article 32 of the Civil Act, which is a nationwide organization with all or part of livestock farmers as its members, and the National Agricultural Cooperatives Federation (including the NongHyup Agribusiness Group; hereinafter the same shall apply) established pursuant to Article 121 of the Agricultural Cooperatives Act;
  4. The term "self-help livestock funds" means funds raised and operated with the amount paid by livestock farmers as their main financial resources in order to promote the development of the livestock industry, such as enhancing the safety of livestock products and promoting the consumption thereof;
  5. The term "compulsory self-help livestock funds" means self-help livestock funds established as the main financial resources of funds compulsorily contributed by livestock farmers (hereinafter referred to as "compulsory contributions");
  6. The term "voluntary self-help livestock funds" means self-help livestock funds established as the main financial resources of funds voluntarily contributed by livestock farmers (hereinafter referred to as "voluntary contributions").

Article 3 (Establishment of self-help livestock funds) (1) Where a livestock organization intends to establish self-help livestock funds (hereinafter referred to as "self-help funds"), it shall choose either a compulsory self-help livestock funds (hereinafter referred to as "compulsory self-help funds") or a voluntary self-help livestock funds (hereinafter referred to as "voluntary self-help funds") for each livestock product.
  (2) A self-help fund shall be installed for 1 livestock product; provided, in cases of beef, individual self-help funds can be established separately for Korean native cattle and beef cattle.
  (3) Where self-help funds are to be established pursuant to paragraph (2), if at least 2 livestock organizations exist for 1 livestock product, such livestock organizations shall jointly establish 1 self-help fund, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 4 (Purposes of self-help funds) (1) Self-help funds shall be used for the following projects:
  1. Public relations through promotion of consumption of livestock products;
  2. Education and provision of information to livestock farmers, consumers, intermediary wholesalers, etc. under Article 18 (2), and collection agencies under Article 19 (1);
  3. Stabilization of autonomous supply of and demand for livestock products, improvement of distribution structure, and promotion of export thereof;
  4. Investigation and research for promotion of consumption of livestock products, improvement of quality and productivity, and improvement of safety thereof;
  5. Evaluation of economic feasibility of self-help fund projects;
  6. Other projects deemed necessary by the compulsory self-help fund management committee under Article 14 or the voluntary self-help fund committee under Article 26 to achieve the purpose of establishing self-help funds.
  (2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), Government contributions or subsidies under subparagraph 2 of Article 6 and subparagraph 2 of Article 25 shall not be used for the purpose of broadcasting under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Broadcasting Act and newspaper advertisements (excluding advertisements in which livestock products are exposed by utilizing them as props in broadcasting programs) under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Act on the Promotion of Newspapers, among projects under paragraph (1) 1.

CHAPTER Ⅱ COMPULSORY SELF-HELP FUNDS

Article 5 (Establishment of compulsory self-help funds) (1) Where a livestock organization intends to establish a compulsory self-help fund, it shall submit a plan for establishing a compulsory self-help fund including the reason for establishment and method of securing financial resource to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs in advance and obtain approval therefor, after at least 2/3 of the representatives elected pursuant to Article 8 (1) casting their votes and consent from at least 2/3 of the voters being obtained, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) A livestock organization shall, without delay, report the results of voting conducted pursuant to paragraph (1) to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs and publish such results within 10 days from the date of voting, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Procedures for and methods of voting under paragraph (1) and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 6 (Financial resources of compulsory self-help funds) Compulsory self-help funds shall be raised from the following financial resources: <Amended on May 25, 2010>
  1. Compulsory contributions;
  2. Government contributions or subsidies;
  3. Subsidies for business operators related to the livestock industry, such as business operators of places of work under subparagraph 11 of Article 2 of the Livestock Products Sanitary Control Act and manufacturers under subparagraph 7 of Article 2 of the Control of Livestock and Fish Feed Act;
  4. Other proceeds from the operation of compulsory self-help funds and other revenues.

Article 7 (Limit of compulsory contributions) (1) The limit of compulsory contributions shall not exceed 5/1,000 of the average transaction price of livestock or livestock products:
  (2) The average transaction price of livestock or livestock products under paragraph (1) shall be based on the average transaction price published by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs; provided, where the market price of the relevant livestock or livestock products has significantly declined due to oversupply or other reasons, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall re-determine the average transaction price by reflecting such decline without delay. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 8 (Election of representatives) (1) Livestock organizations shall elect representatives from among livestock farmers by selecting the Special Metropolitan City, a Metropolitan City, a Special Self-Governing City, a Do, a Special Self-Governing Province, or a Si/Gun/Gu (Gu refers to an autonomous Gu) as electoral districts (hereinafter referred to as "electoral district") for voting, etc. for and against the establishment and abolition of compulsory self-help funds. In such cases, expenses incurred by the livestock organizations in the election of representatives may be paid from compulsory self-help funds. <Amended on Nov. 14, 2011>
  (2) The term of office of the representatives elected pursuant to paragraph (1) shall be 4 years.
  (3) The total number of representatives shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree within the scope of at least 30 but not more than 250, but the distribution of the number of representatives by electoral district shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree, taking into consideration the number of livestock farmers in the relevant electoral districts, the scale of livestock husbandry, etc.
  (4) The election of representatives shall be conducted by a majority of livestock farmers in the electoral district or by livestock farmers breeding or producing at least 2/3 of livestock or livestock products in the electoral district.
  (5) The elected representatives shall be classified pursuant to the following classification:
  1. Where the number of candidates is equal to or smaller than the number of representatives allocated for each electoral district: Election without voting;
  2. Where the number of candidates exceeds the number of representatives allocated for each electoral district: Determination of the candidate with the most votes in the order of the votes received, up to the number of representatives allocated for each electoral district; provided, when 2 or more candidates receive the same number of votes, leading to excess of the number of representatives allocated to that electoral district, the candidate in order of seniority, from among the candidates with the same number of votes, shall be elected.
  (6) In order to elect representatives under paragraph (4), a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu (Gu refers to the head of an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply) shall report to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs on administrative statistics on the number of livestock farmers in the area under his or her jurisdiction and the number of heads of bred livestock by each livestock farmer within the latest 1 year, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. In such cases, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall report thereon through the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayor, or Do Governor. <Amended on Nov. 14, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (7) Matters necessary for the qualifications, registration, and voting of candidates for representatives and the election of representatives shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 9 (Special election of representatives) (1) A livestock organization shall hold a special election if a vacancy occurs among the representatives elected pursuant to Article 8 (1); provided, if the remaining term of office is less than 1 year, the special election may not be held.
  (2) The term of office of the representatives elected pursuant to paragraph (1) shall be the remaining term of office of the predecessors.
  (3) Article 8 (1) and (4) through (7) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the procedures, methods, etc. of the special election under paragraph (1).

Article 10 (Establishment of board of representatives) A livestock organization shall establish a board of representatives comprised of representatives elected pursuant to Article 8 (1) to make decisions related to compulsory contributions.

Article 11 (Composition of board of representatives) (1) The board of representatives shall have 1 speaker, 1 deputy speaker, and at least 2 but not more than 5 auditors.
  (2) The speaker of the board of representatives (hereinafter referred to as the "speaker") shall be elected by the board of representatives from among representatives, and his or her term of office shall be 2 years.
  (3) The deputy speaker of the board of representatives shall be a person recommended by the speaker from among representatives and appointed by the board of representatives, and his or her term of office shall be 2 years.
  (4) The auditors shall be elected by the board of representatives from among representatives, and the term of office of each auditor shall be 2 years.

Article 12 (Operation of board of representatives) (1) The speaker shall represent the board of representatives and control the proceedings.
  (2) The board of representatives shall be convened when the speaker deems it necessary; provided, the speaker shall convene a meeting of the board of representatives within 1 month when requested by at least 1/4 of the registered representatives or at the request of the chairperson of the compulsory self-help fund management committee under Article 14, or at the request of the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs under Article 22 (2). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) A majority of the members of the board of representatives shall constitute a quorum, any resolution thereof shall require the concurring vote of a majority of those present; provided, at least 2/3 of the members of the board of representatives shall constitute a quorum for matters under subparagraphs 1 and 2 of Article 13, and any resolution thereof shall require the concurring vote of at least 2/3 of the members of those present.
  (4) Except as otherwise provided in paragraphs (1) through (3), matters necessary for the operation of the board of representatives shall be determined by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 13 (Duties of board of representatives) The board of representatives shall deliberate on the following matters:
  1. Establishment and abolition of compulsory self-help funds (excluding where compulsory self-help funds are abolished pursuant to Article 23 (3));
  1. Amount of compulsory contributions;
  3. Plans for projects for compulsory self-help funds and settlement of accounts;
  4. Revision to an amount of expenditure in a main category (excluding revision thereto not exceeding 2/10);
  5. Audit of operation of compulsory self-help funds;
  6. Election of members and chairperson of the compulsory self-help fund management committee under Article 15 (1) 1 and (3);
  7. Dismissal of the chairperson of the compulsory self-help fund management committee under Article 22;
  8. Enactment and amendment of regulations on management of the board of representatives;
  9. Other matters the speaker deems it necessary to go through the resolution of the board of representatives.

Article 14 (Establishment of compulsory self-help fund management committee) A livestock organization that has established a compulsory self-help fund shall establish a compulsory self-help fund management committee (hereinafter referred to as the "management committee") for the effective operation of compulsory self-help funds.

Article 15 (Composition of management committee) (1) The management committee shall be comprised of at least 17 but not more than 25 members, including the chairperson and the vice-chairperson, and the members shall be comprised of the following persons. In such cases, a majority of the members shall be representatives under subparagraph 1: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Persons elected by the board of representatives, from among representatives;
  2. Relevant public officials prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs;
  3. The head of a nationwide organization, from among livestock organizations;
  4. The chief executive officer for the livestock economy of the National the National Agricultural Cooperative Federation under Article 126 of the Agricultural Cooperatives Act or a person appointed by such chief executive officer;
  5. The representative of a collecting agency under Article 19 (1) or a person appointed by such representative;
  6. The representative of a non-governmental organization (referring to a non-profit, non-governmental organization under Article 2 of the Assistance for Non-Profit, Non-Governmental Organizations Act) for the purpose of protecting consumers, etc., designated by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, or a person appointed by such representative;
  7. Persons appointed by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs from among experts in academic circles, production, processing, distribution, and public relations marketing recommended by livestock organizations;
  8. Other persons appointed by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs from among persons recommended by the livestock-related industries or institutions prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.
  (2) The management committee shall have 1 chairperson and 2 vice-chairpersons.
  (3) The chairperson shall be elected by the board of representatives from among representatives, and his or her term of office shall be 2 years.
  (4) The vice-chairpersons shall be elected from among representatives, and the term of office of each vice-chairperson shall be 2 years.
  (5) The term of office of the members other than the chairperson and the vice-chairpersons shall be 4 years.
  (6) A member shall hold an honorary position, but he or she may be paid actual expenses, such as expenses incurred in performing his or her duties, as prescribed by the regulations of the management committee.
  (7) A majority of the members of the management committee shall constitute a quorum and any resolution thereof shall require the concurring vote of a majority of those present.
  (8) Except as otherwise provided in paragraphs (1) through (7), matters necessary for the composition and operation of the management committee shall be determined by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 16 (Duties of management committee) The management committee shall perform the following duties in connection with the procurement and management of compulsory self-help funds:
  1. Receipt of compulsory contributions and management and execution of compulsory self-help funds;
  2. Determination of fees related to entrustment of receipt of compulsory contributions;
  3. Formulation and modification of operational plans for compulsory self-help funds, settlement of accounts, and report to the board of representatives on details thereof;
  4. Affairs delegated by the board of representatives;
  5. Enactment and amendment of regulations on management of the management committee;
  6. Matters concerning the operation of the secretariat of compulsory self-help funds.

Article 17 (Establishment of secretariat of compulsory self-help funds) (1) The management committee may establish a secretariat of compulsory self-help funds (hereinafter referred to as "secretariat") to assist the operation and affairs of the management committee.
  (2) The chairperson shall determine matters necessary for operating the secretariat upon resolution of the management committee.

Article 18 (Payment of compulsory contributions) (1) Where a compulsory self-help fund is established, a livestock farmer shall, when slaughtering livestock, selling it for slaughter (including consignment sale; hereafter the same shall apply in this paragraph, paragraph (2), and Article 35 (2) 1 and 2), or selling livestock products, pay the amount of compulsory contributions resolved by the board of representatives to the management committee within 30 days from the date of such slaughter or sale.
  (2) Where a livestock farmer fails to directly pay compulsory contributions pursuant to paragraph (1), the livestock farmer shall have an intermediary wholesaler, meat seller, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "intermediary wholesaler, etc.") pay compulsory contributions to the management committee on his or her behalf. In such cases, the intermediary wholesaler, etc. may claim compulsory contributions from the livestock farmer who sold the relevant livestock for slaughter or sold the relevant livestock products. <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>
  (3) The management committee shall notify livestock farmers of the matters concerning the payment of compulsory contributions, such as persons subject to payment, the amount to be paid, and the method of payment, by utilizing the methods of service of a notice of payment of compulsory contributions or public notice thereof.
  (4) The management committee shall post a notice of payment of compulsory contributions at places easily accessible by livestock farmers, intermediary wholesalers, etc.
  (5) Except as otherwise provided in paragraphs (1) through (4), matters necessary for the payment of compulsory contributions such as the methods for payment of compulsory contributions by item shall be determined by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 19 (Entrustment of collection of compulsory contributions) (1) The chairperson of the management committee may entrust the collection of compulsory contributions under Article 18 (1) and (2) to a person who represents a workplace under subparagraph 11 of Article 2 of the Livestock Products Sanitary Control Act. In such cases, the management committee may pay fees to the representative of the workplace (hereinafter referred to as "collecting agency") entrusted with the collection of compulsory contributions, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on May 25, 2010; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) The collecting agency shall neither refuse a request for collection of compulsory contributions from a livestock farmer or intermediary wholesaler, etc. nor suspend the collection of compulsory contributions without suspension of business, temporary closure of business, or any other good cause prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Where a livestock farmer or intermediary wholesaler, etc. refuses to pay compulsory contributions, the collecting agency may refuse the slaughtering or milk collection of the relevant livestock or livestock products.
  (4) When the collecting agency has collected compulsory contributions, it shall pay the compulsory contributions to the relevant management committee by the 20th day of the following month; provided, where the payment is delayed, it shall pay the delayed amount to the management committee by applying the simple interest rate of 3/10,000 per day to the delayed amount by the period of delayed payment.
  (5) The management committee shall collect compulsory contributions that a livestock farmer or intermediary wholesaler, etc. fails to pay to the collecting agency.
  (6) The collecting agency shall manage compulsory contributions in a separate account.

Article 20 (Refund of overpayment or erroneous payment) When a livestock farmer requests the return of an amount overpaid or erroneously paid by a livestock farmer by mistake, etc., the management committee shall return it without delay, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, and the overpaid or erroneously paid amount verified by the management committee shall be returned, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, even if no request is made by the livestock farmer. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Feb. 11, 2020>

Article 21 (Operation and management of compulsory self-help funds) (1) The compulsory self-help funds shall be operated and managed by the management committee.
  (2) The management committee shall prepare a draft for the operational plan of compulsory self-help funds and obtain approval therefor from the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs following a resolution by the board of representatives. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) The operational plan for compulsory self-help funds under paragraph (2) shall be divided into a revenue plan and an expenditure plan, and the expenditure plan shall be divided into major items and detailed items for each project.
  (4) Where the management committee intends to modify the amount of expenditure in a major item of the operational plan for compulsory self-help funds, it shall prepare a draft of the modified operational plan of compulsory self-help funds and obtain approval therefor from the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs following a resolution by the board of representatives. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (5) Notwithstanding paragraph (4), where the amount of expenditure in the major item is modified by less than 2/10, the management committee may prepare a draft of the modified operational plan of compulsory self-help funds and obtain approval therefor from the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, without resolution by the board of representatives. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (6) The management committee shall disclose matters concerning the procurement and operation of compulsory self-help funds, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (7) Expenses incurred in operating compulsory self-help funds shall not exceed the standards prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs according to the scale of self-help funds raised. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (8) Except as otherwise provided for in paragraphs (1) through (7), matters necessary for the operation and management of compulsory self-help funds, such as the creation of compulsory self-help funds, procedures for formulating and modifying plans for operation, and matters concerning accounting and contracts, shall be determined by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 22 (Dismissal of chairperson of management committee) (1) Representatives may request the board of representatives to dismiss the chairperson of the management committee from office at the request of at least 1/4 of the registered representatives.
  (2) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may request the board of representatives to dismiss the chairperson of the management committee from office in any of the following cases: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Where he or she has been subject to criminal punishment in connection with the operation of self-help funds;
  2. Where deemed necessary to dismiss the chairperson as a result of an inspection conducted under Article 31.

Article 23 (Abolition of compulsory self-help funds) (1) Where a livestock farmer deems it difficult to achieve the purpose of establishing an compulsory self-help fund, he or she may request the board of representatives to abolish the compulsory self-help fund with the signature of at least 1/10 of livestock farmers or livestock farmers who raise or produce at least 1/4 of livestock or livestock products as of the end of the previous year.
  (2) The speaker shall convene a meeting of the board of representatives and hold a vote for and against the abolition of the compulsory self-help fund within 60 days from the date of receipt of the request from the livestock farmer under paragraph (1).
  (3) Notwithstanding paragraph (2), where at least 1/2 of livestock farmers request the abolition of the compulsory self-help fund, the compulsory self-help fund shall be deemed abolished from the time the request is made.
  (4) When a resolution to abolish the compulsory self-help fund is passed pursuant to paragraph (2) or when a request for the abolition of the compulsory self-help fund is made pursuant to paragraph (3), the speaker shall immediately notify the management committee and the collecting agency of such fact and suspend the receipt of compulsory contributions through the management committee or the collecting agency. In such cases, the compulsory contributions paid before the resolution to abolish the compulsory self-help fund is passed pursuant to paragraph (2) or the request for the abolition of the compulsory self-help fund is made pursuant to paragraph (3) shall not be returned, and the compulsory contributions whose payment is delayed shall be paid.
  (5) Upon receiving the request for the abolition of the compulsory self-help fund pursuant to paragraph (1), the speaker shall, without delay, report the grounds for such request, schedule of a vote for and against the abolition of the compulsory self-help fund, etc. to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs and publish such results within 10 days from the date of request for the abolition thereof, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (6) The speaker shall, without delay, report the voting results under paragraph (2) or the fact of abolition of the compulsory self-help fund under paragraph (3) to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs and publish such results within 10 days from the date of voting or the date of abolition, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (7) Where the compulsory self-help fund is abolished, matters necessary for the abolition of the compulsory self-help fund, such as the method of disposing of the remaining amount after the execution of the compulsory self-help fund, shall be determined by the board of representatives.

CHAPTER Ⅲ VOLUNTARY SELF-HELP FUNDS

Article 24 (Establishment of voluntary self-help funds) Where a livestock organization intends to establish a voluntary self-help fund, it shall submit a plan for establishing a voluntary self-help fund including the reason for establishment and method of securing financial resource to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs in advance and obtain approval therefor, and obtain signatures from at least 5/100 of the livestock farmers, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 25 (Financial resources of voluntary self-help funds) Voluntary self-help funds shall be raised from the following financial resources: <Amended on May 25, 2010>
  1. Voluntary contributions;
  2. Government contributions or subsidies;
  3. Subsidies for business operators related to the livestock industry, such as business operators of places of work under subparagraph 11 of Article 2 of the Livestock Products Sanitary Control Act and manufacturers under subparagraph 7 of Article 2 of the Control of Livestock and Fish Feed Act;
  4. Other proceeds from the operation of voluntary self-help funds and other revenues.

Article 26 (Establishment of voluntary self-help funds) A livestock organization that has established a voluntary self-help fund shall establish a voluntary self-help fund committee (hereinafter referred to as the "committee") to deliberate on the following matters:
  1. Amount of voluntary contributions;
  2. Abolition of voluntary self-help funds;
  3. Formulation and modification of operational plans for voluntary self-help funds and settlement of accounts;
  4. Other matters the chairperson deems important and takes into deliberation by the committee.

Article 27 (Composition and operation of committee) (1) The committee shall be comprised of at least 15 members, including the chairperson and the vice-chairperson, and the members shall be comprised of the following persons. In such cases, a majority of the members shall be livestock farmers under subparagraph 1:
  1. A livestock farmer appointed by the head of a livestock organization;
  2. A public official falling under Article 15 (1) 2;
  3. Persons appointed by the heads of livestock organizations from among academic circles, experts in each field of production, processing, and distribution, and consumers.
  (2) The committee shall have 1 chairperson, 1 vice-chairperson, and 2 auditors.
  (3) The chairperson, the vice-chairperson, and the auditors shall be elected by and from among the members, respectively, and their terms of office shall be 2 years, respectively.
  (4) The term of office of the members other than the chairperson, the vice-chairperson, and the auditors shall be 4 years.
  (5) A majority of the members of the committee shall constitute a quorum, any resolution thereof shall require the concurring vote of a majority of those present; provided, at least 2/3 of the members shall constitute a quorum for matters under subparagraph 2 of Article 26, and any resolution thereof shall require the concurring vote of at least 2/3 of the members of those present.
  (6) Except as provided for in paragraph (1) through (5), matters necessary for the composition and operation of the committee shall be determined by the committee.

Article 28 (Operation and management of voluntary self-help funds) (1) Voluntary self-help funds shall be operated and managed by livestock organizations.
  (2) Livestock organizations shall prepare a draft for the operational plan of voluntary self-help funds and obtain approval therefor from the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs after deliberation by the committee. The same shall also apply to modification to the operational plan of voluntary self-help funds. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Livestock organizations shall clarify the accounting of voluntary self-help funds and keep the accounts of the funds separately from other accounts.
  (4) Article 21 (7) shall apply mutatis mutandis to expenses incurred in operating voluntary self-help funds.

Article 29 (Abolition of voluntary self-help funds) (1) Where a livestock farmer deems it difficult to achieve the purpose of establishing voluntary self-help funds, he or she may request the committee to abolish the voluntary self-help funds after obtaining signatures from at least 5/100 of the livestock farmers.
  (2) Article 23 (2) through (7) shall apply mutatis mutandis to votes for and against the abolition of voluntary self-help funds, reporting, publication, etc. of the results thereof. In such cases, "speaker" shall be construed as "chairperson", "board of representatives" as "committee", "compulsory self-help funds" as "voluntary self-help funds", "management committee and collecting agency" and "management committee or collecting agency" as "livestock organizations", respectively, and "compulsory contributions" as "voluntary contributions", respectively.

CHAPTER IV SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 30 (Prohibition of use of similar name) Unless it is a self-help fund under this Act, a self-help fund or a similar name using the term for livestock or their products under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 may not be used.

Article 31 (Guidance and supervision) (1) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall guide and supervise affairs concerning the creation and operation of self-help funds, and may require livestock organizations, the management committee, the committee, and receiving agencies (hereinafter referred to as "livestock organizations, etc.") to report necessary matters concerning the affairs, accounting, property, etc. concerning self-help funds or to submit necessary documents, and may require public officials under his or her jurisdiction to enter offices, etc. to inspect books of account and other documents. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs intends to conduct an inspection pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall notify the livestock organization of the date and time, the purpose of, details, etc. of the inspection by not later than 7 days before the inspection; provided, this shall not apply in urgent circumstances or where he or she is deemed unable to achieve the purpose of the inspection if he or she gives prior notification.
  (3) A public official who conducts an inspection pursuant to paragraph (1) shall carry an identification card indicating his or her authority and produce it to interested parties.
  (4) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may issue orders and take measures necessary to supervise livestock organizations, etc. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Feb. 11, 2020>
  (5) Except as otherwise provided for in paragraphs (1) through (4), matters necessary for the guidance, supervision, etc. shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 32 (Delegation of authority) Part of the authority of the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs under this Act may be delegated to the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, or the Governor of a Special Self-Governing Province, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Nov. 14, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013>

CHAPTER V PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 33 (Penalty provisions) Any person who performs accounting in violation of Article 28 (3) shall be punished by a fine not exceeding 5 million won.

Article 34 (Joint penalty provisions) Where the representative of a livestock organization or an agent, or employee of, or other persons employed by, the livestock organization or an individual commits any violations under Article 33 in connection with the affairs of the livestock organization or individual, in addition to punishing the violators accordingly, the livestock organization or individual shall be subject to a fine under said Article; provided, this shall not apply where such livestock organization or individual has not been negligent in giving due attention and supervision concerning the relevant affairs.

Article 35 (Administrative fines) (1) Any of the following persons shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding 10 million won:
  1. A person who uses self-help funds in violation of uses under Article 4 (1) or (2);
  2. A person who refuses to request the collection of compulsory contributions or suspends the collection of compulsory contributions without good cause, in violation of Article 19 (2);
  3. A person who fails to hold a vote for and against the abolition of self-help funds, in violation of Article 23 (2) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 29 (2));
  4. A person who fails to suspend the collection of compulsory self-help funds, in violation of the former part of Article 23 (4) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 29 (2));
  5. A person who uses the same name as self-help funds or any similar name, in violation of Article 30.
  (2) A person who falls under any of the following subparagraphs shall be punished by an administrative fine not exceeding 3 million won: <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>
  1. A person who fails to pay compulsory contributions within 30 days from the date on which livestock is slaughtered or sold for slaughter or livestock products are sold, in violation of Article 18 (1);
  2. A person who fails to pay compulsory contributions received from a livestock farmer on his or her behalf within 30 days from the date on which livestock is slaughtered or sold for slaughter or livestock products are sold, in violation of the former part of Article 18 (2);
  3. A person who fails to make a disclosure under Article 21 (6) or falsely makes a disclosure;
  4. A person who fails to file a report or submit related documents under Article 31 (1), who files a false report or submits a false document, or who refuses, interferes with, or evades an inspection.
  (3) Administrative fines under paragraph (1) 1, and 3 through 5 and paragraph (2) shall be imposed and collected by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, and administrative fines under paragraph (1) 2 shall be imposed and collected by the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, or the Governor of a Special Self-Governing Province, respectively, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 22, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013>

